---
title: Updating your project
sidebar:
  order: 5
---

Paper Mario DX publishes releases. Each one is a Git tag that ends in a date, in the form `dx-YYYY-MM-DD`. A fix for an older release adds a number after the date, as in `dx-YYYY-MM-DD.1`. To update your project, merge a release into your `main` branch.

If you use Diorama64, it does this for you when you update your mod. The rest of this page is for updating from the terminal.

In VS Code, open a terminal (**Terminal > New Terminal**), then:

## Add papermario-dx as a remote named `papermario-dx`

```sh
git remote add papermario-dx https://knot.starhaven.dev/did:plc:dfsxnyhzxwqpmykryrjtjabz
```

If you cloned your project from GitHub, or added Paper Mario DX as `upstream`, rename that remote and point it here instead:

```sh
git remote rename origin papermario-dx
git remote set-url papermario-dx https://knot.starhaven.dev/did:plc:dfsxnyhzxwqpmykryrjtjabz
```

Use `upstream` in place of `origin` if that's the name you gave it.

## See what's new

Fetch the releases and list them, newest first:

```sh
git fetch papermario-dx --tags
git tag --list 'dx-*' --sort=-creatordate
```

Each release's notes are in its tag. Read them before you merge, since they say what you need to change in your own project:

```sh
git tag -n99 dx-YYYY-MM-DD
```

The same notes are in the [changelog](/papermario-dx/changelog).

## Merge a release into your `main` branch

```sh
git checkout main
git merge dx-YYYY-MM-DD
```

Replace `dx-YYYY-MM-DD` with the release you want. If your project is on an older release, merge the newest release rather than each one in turn. A release includes everything before it.

## Resolve any merge conflicts

VS Code highlights conflicting sections in the editor with buttons like **Accept Current Change**, **Accept Incoming Change**, and **Accept Both Changes**. Stage each resolved file in the Source Control panel (**Ctrl+Shift+G**), then commit the merge.

## Build

Build as usual with **Ctrl+Shift+B**. The first build after an update downloads the toolchain for that release.

## Merging main instead

If you need a change that isn't in a release yet, you can merge `papermario-dx/main`:

```sh
git fetch papermario-dx
git merge papermario-dx/main
```

`main` can hold unfinished work, and its changes aren't in the changelog's release notes until the next release. A toolchain is published for every release but only for the newest commits on `main`. If you merge an older `main` commit, the build can fail with an error that no commit in your history has a published toolchain. Merge a release to fix it.
